_:n8464a9b136a2454fac562b5d86706d30b7 <http://www.loc.gov/mads/rdf/v1#citationNote> "history (In August 1953, the ILA was suspended from the American Federation of Labor. The AFL created the International Brotherhood of Longshoremen (IBL-AFL) to replace the besmirched ILA and scheduled representational elections. By the time an AFL-CIO committee recommended re-admittance for the ILA in August 1959, the IBL was active only in the Great Lakes. In October, the IBL officially dissolved itself)" .
_:n8464a9b136a2454fac562b5d86706d30b9 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.loc.gov/mads/rdf/v1#Source> .
_:n8464a9b136a2454fac562b5d86706d30b15 <http://id.loc.gov/ontologies/bflc/marcKey> "4102 $aILA Local 38-97 (Tacoma, Wash.)$7(bcp47)und-latn"@und-latn .
<http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n2005175678> <http://www.loc.gov/mads/rdf/v1#hasExactExternalAuthority> <http://viaf.org/en/viaf/LC%7Cn%202005175678> .
_:n8464a9b136a2454fac562b5d86706d30b9 <http://www.loc.gov/mads/rdf/v1#citationStatus> "found" .
_:n8464a9b136a2454fac562b5d86706d30b11 <http://www.loc.gov/mads/rdf/v1#citationNote> "(ILWU Local 23 representing Port of Tacoma longshore workers. Local 23 is the last major West Coast longshore local to affiliate with the ILWU. In 1957, the Tacoma local chose to leave the AFL-CIO and accepted an invitation to combine its pension and benefit programs with the ILWU. By the end of the year, Tacoma longshore workers finally voted in favor of seeking a charter from the ILWU)" .
